(vector
 (map
  (:adr/id "adr-2609112500-a-hash-that-does-not-vary-with-the-repository")
  (:adr/title "A hash that does not vary with the repository")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-11")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609112300-a-generator-written-to-stop-a-class-stopped-one-instance"))
  (:adr/body "
## What this iteration did

Regenerated oracle-handlers from each repository's own root spec, the same
treatment oracle-filters got in the previous iteration. Twelve components
landed, verified against their own compiled aarch64 artifact.

## The defect

Selector 6 is the update-SUCCESS arm. Selector 7 is already its 404 case,
and selector 13 hashes a field the update is supposed to have written --
so 6 was always meant to be the 200.

It updated with a field named jurisdiction. No entity in this cohort
declares that except com-aadhaar's, INCLUDING the template's own Part,
whose fields are partNumber, name, revision and material. An unknown field
is rejected, so selector 6 measured 400 everywhere and the 200 path was
never exercised in any repository.

Selector 13 then hashed a field the rejected body does not carry.

## The tell, and the sentinel

Selector 13 read 7 in com-agones and 7 in com-airtable, while selector 14
read 298858833 and 515051998 in the same two. A hash that does not vary
with the repository is hashing a constant -- here the empty string, which
is what field returns for a key the body does not have.

That 7 is worth naming. It is the value this selector reads whenever the
field name is wrong, which is exactly the defect class. So the floor for
the regenerated block is: selector 6 is 200, and selector 13 is not 7.

## Reproduce

    kbb --backend sci scripts/gen-whole-component.cljk <template> <worktree> <src-dir>
    amu compile src/<src>/whole_component.kotoba --target aarch64-macos
      --policy policy.edn --output wc.o
    amu extract-native wc.o --symbol oracle-handlers --output h.bin
    for s in 6 13; do
      KEXE_FUEL=100000000 ./kexe-loader h.bin <offset> 1 aarch64 <caps> $s
    done

    before   sel 6 = 400   sel 13 = 7
    after    sel 6 = 200   sel 13 = a hash of the value written

## Nothing was traded away

The rejection arm stays covered. reject-unknown is ONE function, and
handle-create calls it with the same (fields-of entity); selector 2
already feeds it an unknown key. Making 6 succeed does not remove
coverage of rejection, it restores coverage of success.

## Seen red

Putting a foreign field name back into the regenerated block returns
selector 6 to 400 and selector 13 to 7 -- the exact signature every one of
these components shipped with.

## A control of mine that was wrong

The first control asked whether selector 13 now varies between
repositories, on the reasoning that a constant hash was the tell. It does
not vary: com-agones and com-abb-robotics both read 727477728, because 13
hashes the VALUE written and that value is a literal chosen in the
generator.

The control was mis-specified, not the fix. The discriminating property is
7 against not-7, not per-repo variation: if the generator emitted a field
name the entity does not declare, the update would reject and 13 would
drop back to 7. Recorded because the number looked like the symptom it was
supposed to rule out, and reading it that way would have condemned a
correct change.

## Not fixed, named

com-abb-robotics is the generator's template and its component is NOT a
fixed point of the generator -- the unpatched control leaves 15 added and
4 removed lines, so it carries hand edits and was not machine rewritten.
Its own selector 6 still measures 400. Since the block is now generated
rather than copied, that text no longer propagates to anything.

The other repositories skipped in the previous iteration are skipped here
for the same named reasons: com-aftership, com-adyen and com-airtable
declare no refs on any spec so the generator refuses them outright, and
com-airbus-analytx is hand adapted.

## What is still refused

The three hash-valued selectors of oracle-store. The pagination layer,
read but not yet generated. The harness does not yet compare the handler
layer at all -- this iteration fixed what the component's own oracle
measures, which is the precondition for generating that comparison, not
the comparison itself.
")))
